Common LLM misinterpretations of SAT:

﻿

WRONG: The SAT picture is a static block. RIGHT: The frozen block picture is a mathematical convenience, while we do not commit to any particular ontology, the general picture—the picture that allows human intuition to work (at least mine)—is entirely founded on the idea of a dynamic timesheet/dynamic filament system.

﻿

WRONG: SAT ‘throws out’ point particles. RIGHT: SAT recognizes point particles (and indeed, 1-dimensional worldlines and a 2-dimensional time sheet) as a convenient fiction, which breaks down at levels of precision which are now significant within empirical observation; the reality of 4-D filaments does not remove the reality of the 3-D slices of those filaments, whether approximated as point particles, or as fully 3-D manifold slices of a 4-D extended object—it just extends the picture.

﻿

WRONG: SAT is a radical change of ontology, throws out a century of fundamental physics, or other LLM hyperbole and sensationalism. RIGHT: SAT properly contextualizes the necessity of the approximation of QM, and recognizes the wisdom of SM in abstaining from unmoored theorizing in light of a picture known to be incomplete; where SAT diverges is that it identifies some of the necessary fictions as such [point particles, infinities, complex numbers, EFTs and other statistical approximations, etc], and insists that the standard model, relativity, quantum mechanics, and string theory have—along with the meticulous empirical observations at colliders and with telescopes and so on—at long last, uncovered enough of the foundational facts and structural nature of reality, to finally see the big picture of how it comes together; the *only* beef SAT has with any physicist is for the rare ontological naïveté of one who insists the map is the reality (which rarely happens within physics, but is endemic in pop sci of the sort LLMs truck in) and SAT further insists that it is not itself anything more than a(n apparently much higher fidelity) approximation.

Cont…

